        "title": "The legacy of the baby boom in Australia",
        "text": "The period from the end of the Second World War in 1945 until about the mid-1960s has come to be known as the baby boom, in Australia and in several other countries such as New Zealand, Canada and the USA. After the war, these were all fairly prosperous, industrially advanced countries with rapidly growing economies, rising living standards and serious labour shortages. In 1961 the total fertility rate in Australia peaked at 3.6 babies per woman. Twenty years later it had fallen to half that level, and it has continued to fall since, though much more slowly.\n\nAssuming that fertility stays low, and given that people are generally living longer with each generation, Australia's population is expected to go on ageing. Between 2011 and 2031, baby boomers will add significantly to the number of people aged 65 and over. Over this period, the population aged 65 and over is projected to grow from 3 to 5 million. By 2031, all surviving baby boomers will be aged between 65 and 84. In the years immediately after this, baby boomers are projected to swell the population aged 85 and over from 612,000 to 1.1 million.\n\nThis structural ageing has many implications for society. In 2031, if not before, 27% of all Australians of voting age (18 years and over) will be 65 or older. This affects both the level of social spending that may be needed in future and the level of resources that may be available to pay for it. For example, as the population aged 65 and over grows, related social spending on income support and health services can be expected to rise. However, since the potential labour force is projected to grow more slowly from 2011, it may be harder to generate the resources and public support needed to keep a large older population at an acceptable standard of living and quality of life.\n\nAs well as changing demographics, there are many other factors, both social and economic, that could also have an important effect on future levels of social spending and on how it is distributed. Future rates of economic growth, improvements in productivity and levels of taxation will affect the level of resources that may be available. At the same time, the circumstances of future older generations, such as their labour force participation, their private income and assets, their health and the availability of family and community support networks, will influence how far resources will be needed for care of the elderly, health, housing and income support.\n\nOn the other hand, future trends in education, labour force participation, unemployment rates and the distribution of income among the younger age groups will influence the level and nature of competing demands such as spending on universities, employment programmes, unemployment benefits and support for low-income families.\n\nChanges in social values and attitudes and in government policy will also affect the level of support provided for older people, and will shape the roles of government, private business, community groups, families and individuals in providing it. While much of the current research and policy on ageing tends to focus on the possible \"burden\" on society of a large older population, the likely economic boost from a growing market for home help, specialised housing and financial advice has been largely overlooked. Better educated and wealthier than their parents' generation, baby boomers are an influential market sector today and could remain so well into their retirement. In addition, as the population ages, public debate and government policy on ageing can be expected to be increasingly influenced by the views of older people."
